  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $h(x)=2x$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$",
